    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d238">

                <pb facs="0556" n="534" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg534"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">888.00/4–2850</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Iranian Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Ala</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Assistant Secretary of State for Near Eastern, South Asian, and African
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >McGhee</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-04-28">April 28, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. McGhee</hi>: Two days after our
                    conversation of the 24th,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d238-f1"/> I received a cable
                    from the Prime Minister<anchor n="2" xml:id="d238-f2"/> of which I venture to
                    send you the substance in translation.</p>
                <p>It practically corroborates the points to which I drew your attention. These are
                    incorporated in the précis of my talk with Mr. Raymond Hare of which I handed
                    you a copy.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d238-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>The emphasis is on the deteriorating economic situation and the need for urgent
                    American assistance.</p>
                <p>What do you think of the suggestion I made to you early in January about a survey
                    of the situation by an outstanding American economist designated by the United
                        States?<anchor n="4" xml:id="d238-f4"/> This might be a way to determine the
                    real need of Iran and the best way to cope with the crisis.</p>
                <p>The Secretary of State has, on various occasions, issued statements which have
                    acted as a deterrent to any contemplated foreign aggression or machinations
                    against Greece, Turkey and Iran.</p>
                <pb facs="0557" n="535" xml:id="pg535"/>
                <p>Might it not be opportune at the present critical juncture, to have recourse to
                    the same salutary procedure?</p>
                <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hussein Ala</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d238-a1.xml" xpointer="d238-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d238-f1" n="1" xml:id="d238-f1-content">
                <p>No memorandum of conversation, April 24, has been found in Department of State
                    files, but see the memorandum of conversation, April 26 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >24?</hi>], by Mr. Ferguson, <ref target="#pg526">p. 526</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d238-f2" n="2" xml:id="d238-f2-content">
                <p>Ali Mansur.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d238-f3" n="3" xml:id="d238-f3-content">
                <p>A memorandum of conversation of Ambassador Ala with Mr. Hare, April 12, by Mr.
                    Ferguson is in Department of State file 888.00/4–1250.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d238-f4" n="4" xml:id="d238-f4-content">
                <p>Reference is presumably to the conversation between Ambassador Ala and Mr. McGhee
                    on January 26 which is reported in the memorandum of the same date by Mr.
                    Ferguson, <ref target="#pg447">p. 447</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
